      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which countries give teachers a route to permanent residence?",
            "answer": "<p>Canada does: Express Entry has an education category, renewed for 2026, that includes K-12 teachers. England currently allows settlement after five years on a Skilled Worker visa, though a ten-year default has been proposed. In the Gulf, residence is tied to your job or to long-term renewable visas.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does teaching experience abroad count for Canada?",
            "answer": "<p>For the Express Entry education category, IRCC asks for at least 12 months of full-time experience (or the part-time equivalent) in the last three years in one eligible occupation, and it can be gained in Canada or abroad. You still need provincial certification to teach.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can a J-1 teaching job lead to staying in the USA?",
            "answer": "<p>Not directly. The J-1 Teacher programme is a cultural exchange of up to three years with possible extensions, and you're expected to go home. Some teachers must spend two years at home before certain U.S. visas or a green card under the 212(e) rule.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I get QTS for England if I trained in Nigeria, Ghana or India?",
            "answer": "<p>Not through the apply for QTS service any more: teachers trained in Ghana, India and Nigeria lost eligibility from 9 September 2026, and the Philippines isn't on the list. GOV.UK points other teachers to different routes into teaching in England.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a teaching licence in every country?",
            "answer": "<p>Almost always. Canada licenses through each province or territory, the USA through each state, the UAE requires a teaching licence by law, and England uses QTS.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will my partner be able to work?",
            "answer": "<p>In England, yes. In Canada, a spouse can get an open work permit in select cases, including when the teacher's permit has at least 16 months left. A J-2 spouse in the USA applies for work permission after arriving, and in the UAE or Qatar a spouse needs their own work permit.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do teachers pay income tax in the Gulf?",
            "answer": "<p>The UAE, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Kuwait and Bahrain don't tax employment income today. Oman will tax income above OMR 42,000 a year at 5% from 2028. Canada, the USA and the UK tax salaries.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can teachers get a UAE Golden Visa?",
            "answer": "<p>No federal Golden Visa category names teachers. Dubai and Ras Al Khaimah have run schemes for outstanding educators with strict criteria, and a Golden Visa is a renewable five- or ten-year residence, not permanent residence.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
